  "Question": "The angles of a pentagon are in arithmetic progression. One of the angles, in degrees, must be:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(108\\)",
    "B": "\\(90\\)",
    "C": "\\(72\\)",
    "D": "\\(54\\)",
    "E": "\\(36\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "In an arithmetic progression of five terms, the terms on either side of the middle balance each other. Let the middle angle measure be \\(m\\) degrees and the common difference be \\(d\\) degrees. The five measures are\n\\[\nm-2d,\\quad m-d,\\quad m,\\quad m+d,\\quad m+2d.\n\\]\nTheir sum is \\(5m\\). The interior angles of a pentagon sum to\n\\[\n(5-2)\\cdot180^\\circ=540^\\circ.\n\\]\nThus \\(5m=540\\), giving \\(m=108\\). Since the middle term is one of the angles, an angle must measure \\(108^\\circ\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
